---
title: "FrontierJS — the ripple"
description: "Change one line of the schema and watch what moves — and, just as importantly, what deliberately does not."
render: static
publishes: 0
---
<!--
  The ripple, the request's path, and the counterfactual.

  All three were built from JavaScript on load. Only the first is interactive —
  the eighteen seams and the comparison tables never were — so the whole page is
  prerendered and the island swaps which of the five edits is showing.
-->
<script>
  import RipplePicker from '../islands/RipplePicker.mesa'

  export let data = null

  const d = data ?? { changes: [], hops: [], first: null }
</script>

<!-- ═══ 1. The ripple ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="ripple">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The ripple</p>
    <h1>Change one line.<br>Watch what moves.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Scaffolding generates code once and then drifts. Derivation does not.
      Pick an edit to the schema — the diff appears on the left, and everything
      downstream that moves because of it appears on the right. Including,
      importantly, the things that <em>don't</em>.
    </p>

    <div class="changeset" data-changes role="tablist" aria-label="Schema edits">
      {#each d.changes as c}
        <button class="btn outlined" role="tab" data-id={c.id}
                aria-selected={c.id === d.first}>{c.label}</button>
      {/each}
    </div>

    <div data-ripple>
      {#each d.changes as c}
        <div class="ripple edit" class:is-shown={c.id === d.first} data-id={c.id}>
          <div class="ripple-left">
            <p class="filename">db/schema.lite</p>
            <pre class="code">{#each c.diff as l}<span class="ln {l.kind}">{@html l.html}</span>{/each}</pre>
          </div>

          <div>
            <p class="tally"><strong>{c.moved}</strong> things moved. {c.still} deliberately did not.</p>
            <div class="blast">
              {#each c.hits as h}
                <div class="hit" class:moved={h.moved} class:still={!h.moved}>
                  <div class="hit-top">
                    <span class="badge {h.moved ? 'success' : ''}">{h.moved ? 'moved' : 'unchanged'}</span>
                    <h3>{h.what}</h3>
                    <span class="realm">{h.realm}</span>
                  </div>
                  <p>{h.note}</p>
                </div>
              {/each}
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      {/each}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ 2. The journey ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="journey">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The journey</p>
    <h2>One request, every seam it crosses</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Most frameworks hide this. FrontierJS names each handoff, because a boundary
      you can name is a boundary you can reason about — and every one of these is a
      real function you can open. Here is a single <code>POST /api/leads</code>,
      end to end.
    </p>
    <div class="hops">
      {#each d.hops as h}
        <div class="hop {h.r}">
          <span class="pip"></span>
          <div class="hop-name">{@html h.name}</div>
          <div class="hop-where">{h.where}</div>
          <p class="hop-say">{h.say}</p>
        </div>
      {/each}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ 3. The counterfactual ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="versus">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The counterfactual</p>
    <h2>What the 13 lines replace</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      "You didn't write this" means nothing until you see what you would otherwise
      have assembled — and, more to the point, kept in agreement with itself
      forever after.
    </p>

    <div class="versus" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>The usual assembly</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0 0 .75rem">
          Every one of these is a good library, and most are better at their one job
          than anything here. The cost is not any of them individually — it is that
          your <em>field list</em> gets restated in ten places, in ten dialects.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>What a Node dev reaches for</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Schema &amp; migrations</td><td>Prisma · Drizzle · TypeORM</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Validation</td>          <td>Zod · Valibot · Yup</td></tr>
              <tr><td>HTTP routing</td>        <td>Express · Fastify · Hono</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Authentication</td>      <td>Auth.js · Lucia · Passport</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Authorization</td>       <td>CASL · Oso · hand-rolled middleware</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Row-level access</td>    <td>Postgres RLS · CASL conditions · hand-written <code>WHERE</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>API spec</td>            <td>zod-to-openapi · @fastify/swagger · NestJS Swagger</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Client types</td>        <td>openapi-typescript · orval · ORM-generated types</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Data fetching</td>       <td>TanStack Query · SWR · RTK Query</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Realtime</td>            <td>Socket.IO · Ably / Pusher · raw <code>ws</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm" style="margin:.9rem 0 0; color:var(--ink-mute)">
          Add a field: ten edits across ten files, and nothing tells you when you
          have missed one. The compiler cannot help — these tools do not know about
          each other.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card">
        <h3>The seed</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0 0 .75rem">
          The same ten concerns, stated once. Everything downstream reads from the
          schema, so disagreement is not something you have to police.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Where it lives</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Schema &amp; migrations</td><td>the <code>.lite</code> file, diffed by <code>fli db:migrate</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Validation</td>          <td><code>@email</code> <code>@length</code> <code>@gte</code>, on the field</td></tr>
              <tr><td>HTTP routing</td>        <td><code>model:</code> on the service</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Authentication</td>      <td><code>IAuth</code> — swap the provider, keep the contract</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Authorization</td>       <td><code>@@gate</code>, one level per operation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Row-level access</td>    <td><code>@@allow</code>, compiled into the <code>WHERE</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>API spec</td>            <td>derived from the registered services</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Client types</td>        <td><code>litestone types</code>, same file</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Data fetching</td>       <td><code>createResource</code> — reactive, hooks matching the API's</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Realtime</td>            <td><code>channel:</code> — one announcement point, two fan-outs</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm" style="margin:.9rem 0 0; color:var(--ink-mute)">
          Add a field: one edit. That is the entire pitch.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <h3>The fair comparison — things that already consolidate</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0 0 .7rem">
        Almost nobody wires all ten by hand any more, and pretending otherwise would
        be a strawman. These are the real alternatives, and each is genuinely good at
        collapsing part of that table.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it collapses</th><th>What is still separate</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>tRPC</strong></td>
              <td>Routing, client types and data fetching — types flow end to end with no spec step</td>
              <td>Your schema, validators and authorization are still three declarations. Nothing derives a UI.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Supabase</strong></td>
              <td>Database, auth, realtime, and row-level access via Postgres RLS — the closest thing to this idea in production</td>
              <td>RLS is written in SQL, beside the schema rather than in it; validation and the client layer are yours. Self-hosting is possible but is not the default path.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Wasp · RedwoodJS</strong></td>
              <td>The whole stack, from one config — the same ambition as this project</td>
              <td>The glue is <em>declared</em> in a spec file you maintain, rather than <em>derived</em> from the data. Authorization lives in handlers, not on the model.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm" style="margin:.9rem 0 0; color:var(--ink-mute)">
        The line worth arguing about is narrower than "fewer dependencies": it is
        whether authorization belongs <em>on the data</em>, and whether the glue should be
        written at all. If those two do not matter to you, the options above are mature
        and this one is not.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="margin-top:1.25rem; border-color: var(--color-warning)">
      <h3>And the part that is not done</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin:0">
        Derivation currently stops at the API boundary. Junction derives services from
        a Model; Sierra does not yet derive forms or tables from one — the JSON Schema
        reaches the browser and nothing renders from it. That is the single largest gap
        in the framework, it is tracked openly, and it is the next thing worth building.
        A page that only showed you the wins would be the wrong kind of page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<RipplePicker client:load />

<style>
section { scroll-margin-top: 5rem; padding: 4.5rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
section:first-of-type { border-top: 0; padding-top: 3rem; }

h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.6vw, 2.9rem); line-height: 1.06; letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .25em 0 .3em; }
h2 { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.9rem); letter-spacing: -.02em; margin: 0 0 .35em; }
h3 { font-size: .95rem; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
.eyebrow { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
.lede { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 62ch; }

/* No code palette and no block rules here. A sample is the design
   system's code block wrapping glow's own code element, and
   @frontierjs/css styles both — a rule here would be a second
   answer to what code looks like. */
/* The one thing the design system cannot know: these sit in a dense
   grid, so they take the rung below the block default. */
.code { font-size: var(--text-xs); padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg); }

.btn.outlined { color: var(--ink); border-color: var(--rule-strong); }

/* ── Ripple ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.ripple { display: grid; gap: 1.75rem; align-items: start; }
@media (min-width: 66rem) { .ripple { grid-template-columns: 26rem 1fr; } }
@media (min-width: 66rem) { .ripple-left { position: sticky; top: 5.25rem; } }

.changeset { display: grid; gap: .4rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
.changeset .btn { justify-content: flex-start; text-align: left; font-size: .84rem; padding: .45rem .75rem; }

.ln { display: block; padding: 0 .4rem; margin: 0 -.4rem; border-radius: 4px; }
.ln.del { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-danger) 15%, transparent); }
.ln.add { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-success) 16%, transparent); }
.ln.del::before { content: '- '; color: var(--color-danger); }
.ln.add::before { content: '+ '; color: var(--color-success); }
.ln.ctx::before { content: '  '; }

.blast { display: grid; gap: .9rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr)); }
.hit { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: .85rem .95rem; background: var(--surface-raised); }
.hit.moved { border-color: var(--color-success); }
.hit.still { opacity: .62; }
.hit-top { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; margin-bottom: .45rem; }
.hit-top .realm { font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin-left: auto; }
.hit p { margin: 0; font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
.hit code { font-size: .76rem; }
.tally { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 .9rem; }
.tally strong { color: var(--color-success); font-size: 1.1rem; }

/* ── Journey ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.hops { display: grid; gap: 0; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
.hop {
  display: grid; gap: .15rem .9rem; grid-template-columns: 2.2rem 1fr;
  padding: .7rem 0 .9rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--rule); margin-left: 1rem; padding-left: 1.25rem;
  position: relative;
}
.hop:last-child { border-left-color: transparent; }
.hop .pip {
  position: absolute; left: -.47rem; top: 1rem;
  width: .8rem; height: .8rem; border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--surface); border: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
}
.hop.d .pip { border-color: var(--color-primary); }
.hop.a .pip { border-color: var(--color-info); }
.hop.u .pip { border-color: var(--color-success); }
.hop-name { grid-column: 1 / -1; font-weight: 600; font-size: .92rem; }
.hop-name code { font-size: .82rem; }
.hop-where {
  grid-column: 1 / -1; font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace;
  font-size: .74rem; color: var(--ink-mute);
}
.hop-say { grid-column: 1 / -1; font-size: .84rem; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: .2rem 0 0; }

/* ── Counterfactual ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.versus { display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; }
@media (min-width: 60rem) { .versus { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }

/* All five edits are in the file and one is shown. */
.edit { display: none; }
.edit.is-shown { display: grid; }

.ripple-left .filename {
  font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .75rem;
  color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 .4rem;
}
.changeset { margin-top: 2rem; }
.hit-top h3 { margin: 0; }
</style>
